{
  "title": "CharacterMovementComponent",
  "description": "Moves a character over surfaces: walking along the ground, falling under gravity, launching into the air, climbing walkable slopes and small steps, staying on the ground over small dips, and riding moving platforms. It composes over a KinematicMovementComponent, which stays the only thing that moves the body; while the character is enabled it issues the movement's commands and `KinematicMovementComponent.move` throws. Call move with the walking input for the coming step and launch to throw the character into the air. Whether a jump is allowed - only from the ground, a second time in the air, shortly after leaving a ledge - is the game's decision: grounded and the other contact state are there to make it, and `launch` works anywhere. Every fixed step the world runs, the character integrates gravity, walks along the surface it stands on at the movement's `maxSpeed`, and casts the result through the movement's collide-and-slide. A `PhysicsWorld.step` call that runs several fixed steps integrates each of them and applies the same walking input to all of them, like a movement command. Contact state - which surface supports the character, which walls and ceilings the last step ran into - describes the end of the latest completed step, so a `fixedUpdate` reading it before the world steps sees the pose the character is in. A surface supports the character when it lies within the walkable angle of up and touches the body's underside. Sensors, and colliders the body's filters exclude, never support it. One-way platforms are open `ChainShape`s, which block only from the side their normal points to: the character jumps through one from below and lands on it from above. Dropping through one is a game rule; clear the platform's category from the character collider's filter mask until the character is below it. Standing on a moving body carries the character with the body's velocity at the character's position, including its rotation, but the character itself never rotates. Leaving the support keeps that velocity, and launching from it adds it to the launch. A support moved by teleporting carries nothing.",
  "symbol": "CharacterMovementComponent",
  "kind": "class",
  "subsystem": "physics",
